        ("Use a simple structure", "Name your professional direction, relevant experience or scope, two or three supported capabilities, and the value you create. Write it after your experience section so every claim can be found elsewhere in the document."),
        ("Example: operations", "An operations coordinator might write: 'Operations coordinator with experience in vendor onboarding, inventory controls, and weekly reporting across multi-location teams. Known for clear documentation and dependable follow-through.' Replace the details with your own evidence rather than copying the sentence."),
        ("Example: early career", "A recent graduate can lead with education, projects, and practical tools: 'Computer science graduate with hands-on web application projects using Python, SQL, and Git. Seeking an entry-level role where careful testing and collaborative delivery matter.' Only list tools you can explain."),
        ("Remove empty claims", "Words such as motivated, results-driven, and hardworking are not harmful, but they rarely distinguish a candidate without evidence. Replace them with the work, environment, or outcome that makes the claim credible."),
